{
  "image_name": "0c2f2ab2ed32fcc8aa7b6bc989a6c23e.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how hyperuricaemia leads to gout and its inflammatory consequences. At the top, three visible causes of elevated urate are shown: urate overproduction from purine degradation, reduced gut excretion, and reduced renal excretion. The gut and kidney sections highlight transporters that normally secrete or reabsorb urate, suggesting that altered handling in enterocytes and renal proximal tubule cells contributes to excess circulating uric acid.\n\nThis excess urate leads to formation of monosodium urate (MSU) crystals. The crystals are then sensed by monocytes, providing a danger signal through receptors such as TLR2/4 and triggering intracellular pathways including NF-κB and MAPK. Crystal uptake activates the inflammasome, which promotes conversion of pro-IL-1β to IL-1β. IL-1β is presented as a central mediator that drives endothelial activation, neutrophil recruitment, and broader pro-inflammatory responses, producing an acute gout flare.\n\nThe lower part of the figure shows two possible outcomes. One pathway points to resolution of inflammation, associated with aggregated neutrophil extracellular traps and degradation of inflammatory mediators. The other shows persistent MSU crystal deposition, chronic tissue inflammation, and clustering into a tophus. Overall, the image links urate imbalance to crystal formation, innate immune activation, acute attacks, and chronic gout.",
